.TH PINGINFOVIEW 1 "2026-05-08" "deb repo v1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pinginfoview \- terminal host-ping monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pinginfoview
.RI [ hosts_file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pinginfoview
is an interactive full-screen terminal tool that continuously pings a list of
hosts and displays their status in a live table.
It is inspired by the Windows utility PingInfoView.
.PP
Each host is pinged once per refresh interval (default: 2 seconds).
The display updates after every round and shows per-host success/failure
counts and the first and last timestamps of each state.
.PP
If
.I hosts_file
is not given,
.B pinginfoview
looks for
.I hosts.txt
in the same directory as the script.
.SH HOSTS FILE
The hosts file contains one entry per line.
Each entry may be a hostname, a fully-qualified domain name, or an IP address.
Lines beginning with
.B #
and inline comments (everything after
.BR # )
are ignored.
Blank lines are skipped.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# My network
192.168.1.1     # router
google.com
8.8.8.8         # Google DNS
.fi
.RE
.SH COLUMNS
.TP
.B HOST
Hostname or IP address as listed in the hosts file (truncated to 22 characters).
.TP
.B IP
Resolved IP address (or the address itself for bare IPs).
.TP
.B ST
Status symbol:
.B ✓
(green) = reachable,
.B ✗
(red) = unreachable,
.B ?
= not yet probed.
.TP
.B OK / FAIL
Cumulative count of successful and failed pings since the monitor started.
.TP
.B FAIL: FIRST → LAST
Timestamp of the first and most recent failed ping.
Displays
.B \-
when no failure has occurred.
.TP
.B OK: FIRST → LAST
Timestamp of the first and most recent successful ping.
Displays
.B \-
when no success has occurred.
.SH KEYS
.SS Normal mode
.TP
.B J / Down-arrow
Move the selection cursor down one row.
.TP
.B K / Up-arrow
Move the selection cursor up one row.
.TP
.B D
Remove the highlighted host from the hosts file.
The last remaining host cannot be removed.
A confirmation flash message is shown in the status bar.
Sorting is reset to the first row when the sort column changes.
.TP
.B H
Sort by hostname (default). Press again to reverse direction.
.TP
.B I
Sort by IP address (numeric). Press again to reverse direction.
.TP
.B S
Sort by status (up first). Press again to reverse direction.
.TP
.B T
Sort by failure time, cycling through four modes:
Last\-Fail ↑ → Last\-Fail ↓ → First\-Fail ↑ → First\-Fail ↓.
Press
.B T
again to advance the cycle.
.TP
.B R
Prompt for a new refresh interval in seconds (minimum 1).
.TP
.B E
Open
.I hosts.txt
in
.BR nano .
After saving and exiting, the host list is reloaded and monitoring restarts.
.TP
.B N
Enter scan mode.
.TP
.B Q / ESC
Quit and restore the terminal.
.SS Scan mode
Scan mode ignores the hosts file and pings every address in the local /24
subnet, displaying only responding hosts.
The column layout matches normal mode: HOST, IP, ST, OK\|/\|FAIL counts,
FAIL: FIRST\(->LAST, and OK: FIRST\(->LAST.
Timestamps are tracked per IP from the first ping and persist across refresh cycles.
In snapshot mode, hosts that went down remain visible in red with their timestamps intact.
.PP
Sort keys work the same as in normal mode:
.B H
sorts by resolved hostname (falling back to IP),
.B I
sorts by IP address (default),
.B S
sorts by status delta in snapshot mode or by OK-ping count in plain scan mode, and
.B T
cycles through failure-time sorts: Last\-Fail ↑ → Last\-Fail ↓ → First\-Fail ↑ → First\-Fail ↓.
Pressing a sort key a second time reverses the direction (except
.B T
which advances the cycle).
Sort state is independent from normal-mode sort and resets to IP on each scan entry.
The active subnet is auto\-detected from the machine's default-route interface.
Reverse\-DNS names are resolved once per newly-discovered IP.
.TP
.B J / Down-arrow
Move the selection cursor down one row.
.TP
.B K / Up-arrow
Move the selection cursor up one row.
.TP
.B A
Append the highlighted host to the active hosts file.
If a hostname was resolved via reverse DNS it is recorded as a comment.
Duplicate IPs are silently skipped.
A flash message in the status bar confirms each addition.
.TP
.B P
Toggle snapshot mode (see below).
.TP
.B B
Toggle visibility of unchanged (dim) background hosts.
Only active while in snapshot mode.
When hidden, only new arrivals (bright green) and dropouts (bright red) are
shown, making it easier to focus on changes.
The status bar shows
.B bg:hidden
while background hosts are suppressed; pressing
.B B
again restores them.
.TP
.B N
Exit scan mode and return to normal hosts\-file monitoring.
.TP
.B Q
Quit and restore the terminal.
.SS Snapshot mode
Snapshot mode is entered by pressing
.B P
while in scan mode.
At that moment the set of live hosts is recorded as a baseline.
Subsequent display refreshes colour\-code changes relative to that baseline:
.TP
Bright green
The host appeared after the snapshot was taken (new arrival).
.TP
Bright red
The host was up at snapshot time but is no longer responding.
.TP
Dim
No change from the baseline.
.PP
Hosts that went down after the snapshot remain visible (in red) even after they
stop responding, so no events are missed.
.PP
The status bar shows
.BI [SNAPSHOT\  HH:MM:SS]
with the time the baseline was recorded.
Press
.B P
again to clear the snapshot and resume the normal scan view.
.PP
Press
.B B
to toggle visibility of unchanged (dim) background hosts.
When background hosts are hidden, only new arrivals and dropouts remain visible.
The status bar appends
.B bg:hidden
as a reminder.
.SH SITE DETECTION
When launched via the
.B piv
alias (from the alias repo), the hosts file is chosen automatically by
querying each site's nameserver directly:
.TP
Query
.B 192.168.1.203
for
.I ssb8.local
→ loads
.I ~/deb/hosts-vienna.txt
.TP
Query
.B 192.168.1.198
for
.I pi.hole
→ loads
.I ~/deb/hosts-aigen.txt
.TP
(neither answers)
falls back to
.I ~/deb/hosts.txt
.PP
The query tool is chosen automatically:
.B dig
(1 s timeout) if available, otherwise
.B nslookup
with explicit server, otherwise a raw
.B /dev/tcp
port-53 reachability check.
This makes detection independent of the system's configured DNS resolver.
.PP
The active site is shown in the status bar as
.BR [VIENNA] ,
.BR [AIGEN] ,
etc.
Pass an explicit hosts file path to
.B piv
to bypass detection.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <script_dir>/hosts.txt
Default hosts file.
.TP
.I <script_dir>/hosts-vienna.txt
Vienna network hosts (auto-selected when
.I ssb8.local
resolves).
.TP
.I <script_dir>/hosts-aigen.txt
Aigen network hosts (auto-selected when
.I pi.hole
resolves).
.TP
.I /tmp/pinginfoview_<pid>/
Temporary directory used to exchange state between background ping workers
and the display loop.
Removed on exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run with the default hosts.txt next to the script:
.PP
.RS
~/deb/pinginfoview
.RE
.PP
Run with a custom hosts file:
.PP
.RS
~/deb/pinginfoview /etc/mynetwork/hosts.txt
.RE
.SH NOTES
Pings are sent with a 1-second timeout (\fB-t1\fR on macOS, \fB-W1\fR on Linux).
ICMP ping may require no special privileges on modern Linux kernels
with \fBnet.ipv4.ping_group_range\fR set permissively; on others, run as root
or grant the binary the
.B cap_net_raw
capability.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ping (8),
.BR nano (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the deb repo homelab toolkit.
